# Chapter 1, part 2: the screen of the Editor and the panels.
.h2 The screen [view-screen]
.ix Screen of the Editor
.ix Edit screen
.ix Screen layout
.ix Window layout
The edit screen has the status line at the top, the ruler and the text area
below it, and two optional parts: the expanded status line and the two rows
of function key labels at the bottom.  The terminal version and the X11
version show the same screen.  The table lists the rows from the top to the
bottom.

.table 17 43
Row | What it holds
Top row | The status line: the document, the attribute, the position, the
 | section number, the mode.
Second row | The expanded status line, when Expanded Status Line? is Yes;
 | otherwise the ruler.
Next row | The ruler.
Middle rows | The text area.  Panels and menus open over it.
Bottom two rows | The labels of the function keys, {k:F1} to {k:F12}, six in
 | each row, when Display On-Screen Help? is Yes (the default).
Bottom row | The message line.  It shares the place of the second row of
 | key labels ({n:view-message}).
.end

.h3 The two versions of the screen [view-screen-kinds]
.ix Terminal and X11, differences
.ix Screen, terminal and X11
.ix Screen size
.ix Colors
The terminal version fills the whole terminal: as many rows and columns as
the terminal has (80 by 25 when the terminal reports no size).  The X11 version opens a
window of 80 columns by 50 rows.  Both show the same screen, with the
exceptions in this table.

.table 18 20 20
Feature | Terminal | X11
Size | The terminal's own. | 80 by 50 characters; the
 | A change of size is | window is as wide as the
 | followed at once. | grid; its height may be
 |  | changed and is kept, its
 |  | width not.
Width of the text | At most 80 characters, | At most 80 characters.
 | at least 40. |
Colors | Those of the terminal. | Fixed palette: grey
 | Marker text is cyan. | text on black.
Bold, italic, | Terminal attributes. | Bold and italic faces
 | | with Xft; else tints.
Underline | Terminal attribute. | A line below the cell.
Cursor | The terminal's cursor. | An amber block.
Equations | The label of the | Typeset pictures, if
 | marker. | LaTeX is installed.
Keycap markers | Reverse video text. | Boxes, or pictures.
Labels of the keys | Always the plain | Follow Shift, Ctrl
 | ones. | and Alt while held.
Mouse | Not used; the | The wheel scrolls; a click
 | terminal keeps its | puts the cursor.
 | own selection. |
Closing | {k:Ctrl-Q}. | The window close button
 | | acts as {k:Ctrl-Q}.
{k:Alt-F} keys, | Often kept back by | Arrive as they are.
 | the terminal. |
.end
{n:appterm} and {n:appx11} describe the terminal version and the X11 version
in full, in that order.  The rest of this chapter describes what both have in common, and
points out the difference where there is one.

.h3 The status line [view-status]
.ix Status line
.ix Status line, fields
.ix Attr (status line)
.ix Pos (status line)
.ix Sect (status line)
.ix OVR (status line)
.ix Modified indicator
.ix Asterisk, modified
.ix Mode (status line)
The top row of the screen is the status line, drawn in reverse video.  From
the left it holds the following fields.

.table 12 48
Field | Meaning
Name | The name of the document file, without its directory, in 12 characters.
 | A document that has not been saved under a name shows {c:(new)}.
 | An asterisk {c:*} just before the name means that the document has been
 | changed since it was last saved or loaded.  The asterisk goes out when
 | the document is saved.
{c:Attr:} | The attribute of the character under the cursor: {c:Normal},
 | {c:Bold}, {c:Italic}, {c:Under}, {c:Super}, {c:Sub}, a combination such
 | as {c:Bold Italic}, or {c:Special} for something else, such as a marker.
{c:Pos:} | The position of the cursor in its column, counted in characters
 | from the left edge of the column, starting at 1.  In a headline the tag
 | ({c:2.1 }) counts: at the start of the text of the headline of
 | section 1 the position is 3, not 1.
{c:Sect:} | The number of the section that holds the cursor ({c:2.1.2}).
 | Only in a structured document; an unstructured document has no
 | such field.
{c:OVR} | Shown while Overtype is on ({k:Alt-O} or {k:Alt-F5}).
Mode | At the right end: the kind of editing.
.end

The mode field shows the kind of editing:

.table 22 38
Mode | When
{c:STRUCTURED EDIT} | A structured document is open.
{c:UNSTRUCTURED EDIT} | An unstructured document is open.
{c:SECTION EDIT} | Edit, Section: one section is shown.
{c:TITLE PAGE EDIT} | The title page is being edited (Edit, Title).
{c:EQUATION EDIT} | The text of an Equation marker is being edited.
{c:NOTE EDIT} and the like | The text of a Note, a Comment or another
 | marker that holds blocks is being edited; the word before EDIT
 | is the title of the box.
.end

.note The field Attr shows {c:Special} on a marker.  The fonts are chosen
  when the document is printed, so the status lines show none.

.h3 The expanded status line [view-expanded]
.ix Expanded status line
.ix Expanded Status Line?
.ix Just (status line)
.ix Indent (status line)
.ix Headline (status line)
The second row is the expanded status line, drawn in reverse video like the
first, when the option Expanded Status Line? is Yes ({k:F12}, Global, Options;
the default is No).  It describes the format of the block under the
cursor, whatever its source.  From the left:

.table 12 48
Field | Meaning
{c:Just:} | The justification of the block: Left, Right, Center or Even.
{c:Indent:} | Which lines of the block are indented: None, First, All but
 | First or All.
{c:L:} | The left block indent, in characters.
{c:Headline} | Shown when the block is the headline of a section.
.end

.pic pictures/view-expanded.png 5.4 The expanded status line and the key labels.

.h3 The ruler [view-ruler]
.ix Ruler
.ix Ruler, tab stops
.ix Tab stops, ruler
.ix Block indent, ruler
.ix Left block indent
.ix Tab types
The ruler is the row under the status line (under the expanded status line,
when that is on).  It shows, for the block that holds the cursor, where the
text starts and where the tabs are.  It is drawn at the width of the
screen; the position of the left edge of the block is the zero of the ruler,
so that the marks stand over the cells of the text that they refer to.

.table 10 50
Mark | Meaning
{c:.} (dim) | An ordinary cell.  A row of them is the baseline of the ruler.
A square | The left block indent (the setting L): one square for each
 | character of the indent.  There is none when the indent is 0.
{c:>} (a right triangle) | A left tab stop.  The same mark stands for a tab
 | of an unknown type.
A left triangle | A right tab stop.
{c:^} | A center tab stop.
{c:.} (bold) | A decimal tab stop.
{c:,} | A comma tab stop.
{c:#} | A number tab stop.
.end

A document that has no tabs of its own has the default one: a left tab
every five characters.  The ruler changes as the cursor moves from block to
block, and as the format of the block is changed ({k:F6}, {k:Ctrl-H}).

.h3 The text area [view-screen-text]
.ix Text area
.ix Block indicator
.ix Section indicator
.ix End of line mark
.ix Tab mark
.ix Dots for spaces
.ix Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters?
.ix Display Dots for Spaces?
.ix Marker, appearance on screen
The text area shows and edits the document.  The Editor wraps the lines,
sets the tabs, justifies and lays out the columns, and shows as much of the
result as fits.  The area scrolls when the cursor leaves it at the top or at
the bottom, so that the cursor is always in view.

The marks of the elements are these:

.table 19 41
On the screen | Meaning
A single line | The border between two blocks.
A double line | The border above a headline: between two sections.
A dotted vertical line | The border between two columns of a block.
A shift of 3 characters | One level of depth in a structured document: the
 | blocks of a section are shifted 3 characters to the right of the
 | blocks of the section above it, for each level.
A left triangle | The end of a line, made with {k:Enter}.
A right triangle | A tab.  The text that follows starts at the tab stop.
A dot for each space | If Display Dots for Spaces? is Yes.
{c:[ ... ]} in cyan | A marker, drawn as its name and value, such as
 | {c:[WS 0.3 in]}.  By default a note is drawn as {c:[NOTE]}.
Reverse video | The highlighted text, column, block or section, and the
 | keycap markers in a terminal.
Bold and italic text | Text with that attribute.  A headline is bold.
.end

The two options that control the marks are on the Options panel of the
Global menu: Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters? (Yes by default) and
Display Dots for Spaces? (No by default).  Both are saved in the file
{c:~/.strata/config} with the other options of the Editor.  The way that
each kind of marker is shown, as a name, as a value, as a diamond, or not at
all, is set by the Marker Display Modes of the Global, Marker panel (see
{n:format} and {n:markers}).

.pic pictures/view-keycap.png 5.4 Markers in the text (X11 version).
The picture shows keycaps drawn as boxes, equations in the line of text, a
note as {c:[NOTE]}, a cross-reference in color and a whitespace marker in
brackets.


.h4 A terminal screen, as text [view-term-screen]
The terminal version draws the same kind of screen (the text of the
screen, 25 rows):

.code
 report.doc   Attr: Normal       Pos: 3   Sect: 1              STRUCTURED EDIT
................................................................................
1 Quarterly report<
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This report describes the work of the third quarter and the plan for the
fourth.  It is divided into three parts, and each part into short sections so
that a reader can see the shape of the whole by looking at the headlines.<
================================================================================
2 Results<
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The quarter ended with all the main goals met.  The details follow.<
   =============================================================================
   2.1 Sales<
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
F1: Help     F3: Copy      F5: Paste      F7: Move       F9: Search  F11:
F2: Split    F4: Cut       F6: Format     F8: Table      F10:        F12: Menu
.end
The less-than sign at the end of the lines stands for the triangle that
marks an end of line, and the rows of minus signs and equal signs for the
single and double lines.

.h3 The labels of the function keys [view-keylabels]
.ix Function key labels
.ix Display On-Screen Help?
.ix On-screen help
.ix Key labels
.ix Modifier keys, labels
When Display On-Screen Help? is Yes (the default) the two bottom rows show
the labels of the twelve function keys, drawn in reverse video:
{k:F1}, {k:F3}, {k:F5}, {k:F7}, {k:F9} and {k:F11} on the first row, and
{k:F2}, {k:F4}, {k:F6}, {k:F8}, {k:F10} and {k:F12} on the second.  Each
entry is the name of the key and its label, for example {c:F3: Copy}.  A key
that has no command has an empty label: {k:F10} and {k:F11} alone and with
{k:Alt}, and every key while {k:Shift} is held.  Every entry has the same place in all
the modes, and two entries are never joined: at least one blank lies between
the end of an entry and the start of the next.  In a terminal that is
narrower than 80 columns the rows are cut at the right edge and the last
entries are lost; they never wrap to the next row.

.table 10 14 16 16
Key | None | Ctrl | Alt
{k:F1} | Help | Learn |
{k:F2} | Split | Math | Append
{k:F3} | Copy | Lib Copy | Join
{k:F4} | Cut | Lib Cut | Delete
{k:F5} | Paste | Lib Paste | Overtype
{k:F6} | Format | Case | Set Attr
{k:F7} | Move | Prev Error | Go To
{k:F8} | Table | Next Error | Border
{k:F9} | Search | Again | Replace
{k:F10} |  | Undo |
{k:F11} |  | Redo |
{k:F12} | Menu | Marker | Marker
.end

In the X11 version the labels change while a modifier key is held: while
{k:Alt} is down they show the Alt column, while {k:Ctrl} is down the Ctrl
column, and while {k:Shift} is down empty labels, because no key has a command
with Shift.  When several are held Alt wins over Ctrl, and Ctrl over Shift.  They go back to the
plain labels when the key is released.  A terminal does not report a modifier
key alone, so the plain labels are always shown.

.h3 The message line [view-message]
.ix Message line
.ix Messages, on screen
.ix Error message, line
The bottom row of the screen reports what was done, and what was refused.  The message is shown in bold, in reverse video.  It replaces
the second row of the key labels (when the labels are on) or appears on a row
of its own (when they are off).  A message stays until the next key is
pressed.  Examples are {c:Overtype on}, {c:Cancelled},
{c:Found}, {c:Not found: abc}, {c:3 replacements}, {c:Hanging indent set at
4}, {c:Only a structured document has sections}, and the text that asks for a
highlighted area: {c:Highlight the area with the cursor keys, then press
Enter (Esc cancels)}.  {X:messages} lists the messages of all the programs
with their meaning.

An error that must be read before work continues (a file that cannot be
written, a marker that is not valid) is shown in a box in the middle of the
screen, headed with the name of the command.  The box waits for a key; the
message {c:Press any key} is at its foot.

.h3 The cursor [view-cursor]
.ix Cursor
.ix Cursor, shape
The cursor is a block (the terminal's cursor in the terminal version, an
amber block in the X11 version).  While a menu or a box is open the Editor
hides it.  On a picture (an equation, a keycap) the X11 version draws a
frame around the picture.

.h2 Panels, menus and prompts [view-panels]
.ix Panels
.ix Prompts
.ix Menus, pop-up
.ix Boxes
.ix Dialog boxes
Strata uses a few kinds of box, drawn over the text, with the same keys in
all of them.  Every box has a border and a title; most have a line at the
foot that names the keys that leave it.  {k:Esc} cancels (the command does
nothing) and the key named at the foot accepts.  A box that is opened from
another box returns to it when it closes.

.table 15 45
Kind | Where it is used
List of choices | The unit menu of Copy, Cut, Move and Delete, the Append,
 | Split and Join menus, the Set Attributes menu, the Go To menu, and
 | every list of names.
Prompt | A line of text: Search, Replace, a file name, a section number.
Panel | A form of fields: the panels of File, Global, Print and Marker.
Grid of commands | The Marker Commands menu (Alt-F12).
Checklist | The names of files, directories or choices of a field.
Message box | An error or a notice, until a key is pressed.
Question | A list of Yes and No, or of ways to go on.
Pager | A scrolling text: the screen of a long result.
Help | The Help ({k:F1}).
.end

.h3 Lists of choices [view-list]
.ix List of choices
.ix Choice list
.ix First letter, selecting
A list of choices is a box with one name on each line.  The highlighted line
is drawn in reverse video.  The keys:

.table 13 47
Key | Effect
{k:Up}, {k:Down} | Move the highlight one line.  It does not wrap.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first, the last entry.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | One page of entries up or down, when the list is longer
 | than the box.
{k:Enter} | Choose the highlighted entry.
{k:Esc} | Cancel; no entry is chosen.
A letter | Move to the next entry that begins with that letter, and in a
 | list of twelve entries or fewer choose it at once.  In a longer list
 | the highlight only moves, and {k:Enter} chooses.
.end
The list scrolls when it is taller than the screen allows; the box is
never wider than the screen minus two cells.  The entry that was chosen the
last time is highlighted again in lists that remember it (Append and Split
start on their second entry).

.h3 Prompts [view-prompt]
.ix Prompt
.ix Input line
.ix Field, text
A prompt is a box with a label, a line for the answer and the reminder
{c:Enter accepts, Esc cancels}.  The line starts with the old answer
(the last search string, say) shown in reverse video.  The first character
that you type replaces all of it; to change the old answer instead, press
{k:Left}, {k:Right}, {k:Home} or {k:End} first, and the highlight goes off.

.table 13 47
Key | Effect
Printing key | Insert the character at the cursor (replace the old answer
 | when it is still highlighted).
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | Move the cursor along the answer.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The start, the end of the answer.
{k:Backspace} | Delete the character before the cursor, or the whole old
 | answer when it is still highlighted.
{k:Del} | Delete the character at the cursor.
{k:Enter} | Accept the answer.
{k:Esc} | Cancel the prompt.
.end

.h3 Panels [view-form]
.ix Panel
.ix Panel, fields
.ix Field
.ix Yes/No field
.ix Choice field
.ix Ins accepts
.ix Ins (accept a panel)
.ix Checklist
.ix Keypad + (list of choices)
.ix Space (toggle a choice)
.ix Information field
A panel is a box of fields, one to a line, each with its label on the left
and its value on the right.  The label is in bold, followed by a colon.
The field that has the focus is drawn in reverse video.  A panel has the
reminder {c:Ins accepts, Esc cancels} (a panel that is one of several has
{c:PgUp/PgDn panels} as well).  There are three kinds of field:

.table 13 47
Kind | What it is
Text | A line of text.  An empty text field is drawn as a row of dots.
Choice | One entry of a short list, drawn in square brackets:
 | {c:[Yes]}.  Changed with {k:Space} (the next entry), {k:Left} and
 | {k:Right} (the previous, the next), or from a list.
Information | A value that is shown and cannot be changed.  It is skipped
 | when you move.
.end

.h4 The keys of a panel [view-form-keys]
.table 19 41
Key | Effect
{k:Up}, {k:Shift-Tab} | Go to the previous field.
{k:Down}, {k:Tab}, {k:Enter} | Go to the next field.  Enter does not accept
 | the panel.
{k:Ins} | Accept the panel.  Its checks are made first; a message box
 | shows what is wrong and the panel stays.
{k:Esc} | Cancel the panel; nothing is changed.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | In a panel that is one of a ring (Global, Marker), go
 | to the next or the previous panel.
{k:Space} | In a choice field: the next entry.
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | In a choice field: the previous, the next entry.
 | In a text field: move the cursor.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | In a text field: the start, the end of the text.
{k:Keypad +}, {k:Alt-+}, {k:F2} | In a text field with a checklist: the files
 | or directories.  In a choice field: the list of its entries.
Printing key | In a text field, insert (the first key replaces the whole
 | old value, which is highlighted).
{k:Backspace}, {k:Del} | In a text field, delete (the first key clears it).
.end

.note The keys + - * of the numeric keypad are used for the outline and for
  the lists of panels, and Strata gives them the meaning of {k:Alt-+},
  {k:Alt--} and {k:Alt-*}.  The X11 version does that by itself for the keypad
  keys.  A terminal sends a plain plus, minus or star for them, which would
  be typed as text, so in the terminal version press {k:Alt} with the key
  ({k:Alt-+}; {k:Alt-=} also works), or use {k:F2} in a panel.  The manual
  writes {k:Keypad +} where it means this key.

.h4 Checklists [view-checklist]
.ix Checklist of files
.ix Checklist of directories
.ix File list in a panel
.ix Directory list in a panel
A text field that names a directory or a file has a checklist, which you
open with {k:Keypad +} or {k:F2} while the cursor is in the field.  The list
shows the directories and the files of one directory:

.pic pictures/view-pick.png 5.4 The checklist of directories.

.table 19 41
Entry | Effect of Enter
{c:[Use this directory]} | In a list of directories: take the directory that
 | the title of the box names.
{c:../} | Go to the parent directory.
{c:name/} | Go into that directory.
{c:name} | In a list of files: put that file name in the field, and, if
 | the panel has a directory field first, the directory in it.
.end
A list of files is of the directory in the first field of the panel, and
shows only the files with the suffixes that the field expects (for a
document, {c:.doc}, {c:.set} and for Save also {c:.bak}).  A name in the
usual directory stays a bare name; a name in another directory is put in the
field with its path.  {k:Esc} closes the list and changes nothing.

.h3 The Marker Commands grid [view-grid]
.ix Marker Commands grid
.ix Grid of commands
.ix Press Ins to Select a Command
The Marker Commands menu, which {k:Alt-F12} and {k:Ctrl-F12} open, is a
grid of names in three columns filled from top to bottom, with the foot line
{c:Press Ins to Select a Command}.  The keys are {k:Up} and {k:Down} (they
wrap round), {k:Left} and {k:Right} (a column to the left or to the right),
{k:Home} and {k:End} (the first, the last name), a letter (the next name that
begins with it; the parenthesis before a name is skipped), {k:Ins} or
{k:Enter} to choose and {k:Esc} to cancel.  A name in parentheses is not
available where the cursor is ({n:view-markers}).

.pic pictures/view-marker-grid.png 5.4 The Marker Commands grid (Alt-F12).
In the picture Section and Page Number are in parentheses because they are
only for headers and footers.

.h3 Questions and message boxes [view-question]
.ix Question
.ix Yes/No question
.ix Message box
.ix Press any key
.ix Unsaved changes, question
A question is a list of choices whose title is the question, such as
{c:Document has unsaved changes} with the entries {c:Save and quit},
{c:Discard changes and quit} and {c:Cancel}, or {c:File does not exist.
Create it?} with {c:Yes} and {c:No}.  Choose with {k:Up}, {k:Down} and
{k:Enter}, or by the first letter, and {k:Esc} cancels the question (it
counts as No, or as Cancel).  A message box shows a notice, with {c:Press
any key} under it, and any key closes it.

.pic pictures/view-save-quit.png 5.4 A question: Quit with unsaved changes.

.h3 The pager [view-pager]
.ix Pager
A pager shows text that is longer than the screen, with the title in a
reverse video row at the top and the line number at its right end.  The keys
are {k:Up}, {k:Down}, {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} (also {k:Space}) to scroll,
{k:Home} and {k:End}, and {k:Esc}, {k:Enter}, {k:Q} or {k:Ctrl-Q} to
close it.

.h3 The menu bar [view-menubar-short]
{k:F12} opens the menu bar in the two top rows.  It is described
in {x:view-menu}.

.h3 Highlighting an area [view-highlight]
.ix Highlight
.ix Highlighting an area
.ix Unit menu
.ix Text, Column, Block, Section (unit menu)
.ix Enter (accept the highlight)
.ix Esc (cancel the highlight)
The commands Copy ({k:F3}), Cut ({k:F4}), Move ({k:F7}), Delete ({k:Alt-F4}),
Lib Copy and Lib Cut ({k:Ctrl-F3}, {k:Ctrl-F4}) and Autosort work on an area
that you mark first.  The command prompts for the unit of the document you
mean, in a small list:

.pic pictures/view-unit.png 5.4 The unit list of the Copy command.

.table 12 48
Unit | The area it marks
Text | Characters, from the cursor to where you move it.
Column | One or more columns of a block.
Block | One or more blocks.
Section | One or more sections (a structured document only).
.end

After the choice the Editor is in highlight mode: the message line shows
{c:Highlight the area with the cursor keys, then press Enter (Esc
cancels)}.  The cursor keys now stretch the highlighted area from the point
where you chose, and the highlighted cells are drawn in reverse video.

.table 17 43
Key | In highlight mode
Cursor keys | Stretch the area (all the movement keys, including
 | {k:Shift-Up} and {k:Shift-Down} for sections).
{k:Enter} | Accept.  The command is carried out.
{k:Esc} | Cancel.  The message {c:Cancelled} is shown and nothing happens.
{k:F1} | Help, and then back to the highlight.
Any other key | The message "Highlighting: move the cursor, Enter to accept,
 | Esc to cancel".  The key has no other effect.
.end
In Autosort (Tools, Autosort, Table) {k:Home} and {k:End} go to the start
and the end of the section, and {k:+} and {k:-} change the sort direction.

.pic pictures/view-highlight.png 5.4 Text highlighted by Copy.

The choices of the unit list differ from command to command only in the
title.  An unstructured document does not offer Section.  The area is
carried out when {k:Enter} is pressed: Copy keeps it for Paste ({k:F5}), Cut
removes it, Move moves it in the place with the cursor keys ({n:edit-b-move}),
and Delete removes it for good.  {X:edit} describes each command.

.h3 The Help [view-help-viewer]
.ix Help
.ix F1 (help)
.ix Help topics, index
.ix Help, links
{k:F1} shows the Help, one topic at a time, in a window across the foot of
the screen.  The first line of the window is {c:HELP: topic name}, the last
line lists the keys.  {x:view-help} describes it.  When no help file is found,
{k:F1} opens a box with the text {c:Help file not found (use --help-file or the
STRATA_HELP variable)}; {n:str-help-search} says where the programs look for the
file.
